----- TEXT -----
The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight is made up of all colors of the visible spectrum, and when it enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with tiny gas molecules—mostly nitrogen and oxygen—that are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light. Rayleigh scattering affects short wavelengths far more strongly than long ones, and it scales roughly with the inverse fourth power of wavelength. This means blue and violet light scatter about ten times more efficiently than red light. As a result, scattered blue light bounces in every direction across the sky, so when you look up, you're seeing blue light that has been redirected toward your eyes from all parts of the atmosphere.

You might wonder why the sky isn't violet, since violet has an even shorter wavelength than blue. Two factors resolve this. First, the Sun emits more blue light than violet light. Second, our eyes are more sensitive to blue than to violet. The combination means the scattered light we perceive is dominated by blue
